ous_frame` to `current_frame`, or `last_transition.before_frame` to `last_transition.after_frame`; `history[-1].frame` is the current frame, not the previous one.\nUse Python to inspect the evidence, refine that world model from the newest history, and search or score candidate actions or short sequences against the current goal as you currently understand it.\nMaintain a compact working world model of what the current level seems to contain, what actions appear to do, what the goal seems to be, what is still uncertain, and what plan currently looks best.\nBelow you are provided with the current world model from the previous turn. The default behavior is to copy it and add or remove things based on the evidence that you gathered. BEFORE EXECUTING NEW ACTIONS YOU MUST ALWAYS GIVE THE REVISED VERSION OF THE WORLD MODEL.\nYou may call `action(actions)` more than once in one Python snippet if your search or control loop needs it, but stop immediately if a result reports `game_over`, `level_completed`, or `done`.\nWorking world model carried from earlier turns:\n- World model: Level 1 has 8 objects on a 64x64 grid. Orange background (id=1) fills most of the board. Green bar at top (row 0). Charcoal block (id=2, 3x4) at rows 1-3, cols 36-39. Magenta block (id=3, 4x4) below it at rows 4-7. Blue bar (id=4, 4x20) at rows 16-19, cols 12-31 - likely the player/character. Two identical yellow blocks (ids 5,6, each 4x20) near bottom at rows 52-55. Light gray bar at bottom (rows 60-63). The blue bar is enclosed by orange.\n- Goal model: Likely navigate blue bar to touch the magenta block (rows 4-7, cols 36-39) or yellow blocks.\n- Action model: Arrow keys move the blue bar 4 cells in the respective direction.\n- Plan: Test UP again from rows 16-19 to see if it still works. Also explore LEFT.\n- Revise any item above immediately if `current_frame` or `history` contradicts it.\nend of world model. \nFocus on what changed most recently in `history`, update the target environment change if needed, and separate gameplay-object changes from HUD-only changes.\nWhen ready, call `action(actions)` from inside the `python` tool with the best valid action or ordered batch selected by your code. If your code has found a reliable short sequence, prefer batching it in one call.\nYou may call `action(actions)` more than once in one Python snippet if your search or control loop needs it.\nIf you include assistant text before a tool call, keep it short and use it to update the world model. Helpful optional prefixes are `World model:`, `Goal model:`, `Action model:`, `Recent findings:`, `Open questions:`, `Plan:`, and `Cross-level notes:`.\nWhen calling `python`, emit exactly the tool-call format shown elsewhere in this prompt for this model. Use only that format; do not add markdown fences, prose wrappers, or alternate tool-call syntax. Do not quote or place tool-call markup inside explanatory text; when you decide to call the tool, emit the tool call itself.\nIf you use MOUSE, include integer row and col arguments.\nEFFICIENCY BUDGET \u2014 your score on each level is (baseline_actions / your_actions)^2, so every wasted action costs you quadratically.\nLevel 1: you have used 8 of about 39 target actions so far.", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}, {"label": "THINKING", "content": "UP worked from rows 16-19 to rows 12-15. So UP does work from rows 16-19. But earlier, when I tried UP from rows 12-15 (after the first UP), it was blocked. So UP works from rows 16-19 but not from rows 12-15. This means the blue bar can only move between rows 12-15 and rows 16-19, not further up.\nWait, but the blue bar started at rows 16-19 and the first UP moved it to rows 12-15. Then subsequent UPs from rows 12-15 were blocked. So the blue bar can toggle between rows 12-15 and rows 16-19 with UP/DOWN.\nBut what about moving further down? Let me check. The yellow blocks are at rows 52-55. Can the blue bar reach them?\nLet me also think about what the goal might be. The blue bar is 20 cells wide (cols 20-39). The magenta block is at cols 36-39, rows 4-7. The blue bar at rows 12-15 overlaps horizontally with the magenta block (cols 36-39). But the blue bar can't reach rows 4-7.\nMaybe the goal involves the yellow blocks? They're at rows 52-55, cols 16-19 and 40-43. The blue bar is 20 cells wide. If I move it down and position it correctly, maybe it can interact with the yellow blocks.\nLet me try moving DOWN multiple times to see how far down the blue bar can go.", "kind": "reasoning", "inContext": false}, {"label": "ASSISTANT", "content": "World model updated: UP works from rows 16\u219212 but is blocked from rows 12\u21928. The blue bar toggles between rows 12-15 and 16-19 with UP/DOWN. Currently at rows 12-15, cols 20-39.\nPlan: Test how far DOWN the blue bar can go - maybe it can reach the yellow blocks at rows 52-55.", "kind": "reasoning", "inContext": true}, {"label": "TOOL CALL: python", "content": "<tool_call>\n<function=python>\n<parameter=code>\n# First go down to rows 16-19, then keep going down\nresult = action(['DOWN', 'DOWN', 'DOWN', 'DOWN', 'DOWN'])\nseg = current_frame.segmentation\nblue = [n for n in seg['nodes'] if n['color'] == 'b'][0]\nprint(f\"Blue bar after 5x DOWN: {blue['boundary']}\")\nprint(f\"gameplay_changed: {result.get('gameplay_changed')}, no_impact: {result.get('no_impact')}\")\n\n# Check all transitions from this batch\nfor i, t in enumerate(transitions):\n    if t.after_frame.step >= current_frame.step - 5:\n        before_blue = [n for n in t.before_frame.segmentation['nodes'] if n['color'] == 'b']\n        after_blue = [n for n in t.after_frame.segmentation['nodes'] if n['color'] == 'b']\n        print(f\"  T: before={before_blue[0]['boundary'] if before_blue else 'N/A'} after={after_blue[0]['boundary'] if after_blue else 'N/A'} no_impact={t.result.get('no_impact')}\")\n</parameter>\n</function>\n</tool_call>", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}, {"label": "TOOL RESULT: python", "content": "Blue bar afte